## Prod Postgres from a laptop (temporary TCP proxy)

Railway `DATABASE_URL` points at the **private** host
(`*.railway.internal`). That is correct for `api` / `agent` / `app` on the
Railway network, but a laptop cannot reach it — `railway run` alone still
fails with "Can't reach database server".

For one-shot prod data work (repairs, invites, restores), open a **temporary
TCP proxy**, rewrite the URL host/port locally, run the script, then **delete
the proxy**. Do not leave a public Postgres proxy up.

```bash
# 1. Open (only while you need it)
railway tcp-proxy create --port 5432 --service Postgres --json
# → domain + proxyPort (e.g. tokaido.proxy.rlwy.net:15025)

# 2. Run (from apps/api) — injects secrets via railway run, swaps host/port
MM_PROXY_HOST=… MM_PROXY_PORT=… railway run -s api -- \
  bun run scripts/run-via-tcp-proxy.ts ./fix-sage-website-notes.ts --dry-run
# then without --dry-run; or ./ensure-user.ts --email … --name "…"

# 3. Close
railway tcp-proxy delete <proxy-id> --service Postgres --yes
railway tcp-proxy list --service Postgres --json   # expect []
```

Helper: `apps/api/scripts/run-via-tcp-proxy.ts`. Used for Sage website repair
and user invites when signup is disabled. Prefer concurrent/batched script
writes — interactive Prisma transactions of 100 rows time out over the proxy.

